//@ run-pass
//@ requires: lean_single
//@ expect-stdout: option box across call: PASS
// An Option<T> that matches Some in the caller must still match Some inside the
// function it is passed to. lean_single gets this right, which is why this test
// passes here. MADAROS DOES NOT -- gated to lean_single below so a Madaros job
// does not report this as a fresh regression.
//
// UPDATE 2026-09-22, current PR head: the specific "None arm matches every
// Option" symptom described below predates this PR's Option/Box payload-type
// fix in self-hosted/ir/lower.sio and may no longer be the live failure mode.
// Measured just now against a from-source Madaros build (fa9b93b31c +
// uncommitted round-16 fixes): `madaros run` on this file no longer returns
// wrong output -- it segfaults (rc=139) during lowering, after
// "typecheck ok", at "lower_array: seed_begin". Different crash, same
// practical result: this test does not pass under Madaros yet. Left
// `//@ requires: lean_single` rather than rewriting the diagnosis below,
// since the segfault has not itself been root-caused.
//
// Measured on the same source, and each line rules something out:
//
//   ordinary i64 argument                    arrives correctly
//   Option passed in and RETURNED unchanged  caller matches Some
//   match on the Option parameter            None
//   copy the parameter to a local, then match None
//   Option<i64> instead of Option<Box<T>>    None
//   Option as second argument                None
//
// The second line is the important one: the value crosses into the callee
// intact, so this is not argument passing. The ABI code is generic over
// scalars and never mentions sum types, and the IR lowerer binds the parameters
// correctly -- SOUNIO_LOWER_LIVE_TRACE=1 shows the right param_count for each
// function. What fails is every match on that value once inside, and copying it
// to a local first does not dodge it.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS. lex_file_parse_items ends in reverse_item_list_loop(items,
// None); under Madaros that loop takes the None arm immediately and returns the
// empty accumulator. The modular parser therefore returns ZERO items for every
// file -- measured on hello.sio: token_count=17, tok[0]=Fn, the item loop runs,
// the item is appended, the list entering the reversal is non-empty, and what
// comes out is empty with errors=0. Both k2_knowledge_unit_constraint_probe and
// k2_unit_derived_ast_probe are built on it, so unit_types_derived_gate and
// knowledge_context_unit_gate have been validating empty lists and have never
// tested their subject.
struct Node {
    v: i64,
}

fn takes_one(current: Option<Box<Node> >) -> i64 with Mut, Alloc {
    match current {
        None => 0
        Some(n) => 1
    }
}

fn takes_two(current: Option<Box<Node> >, acc: Option<Box<Node> >) -> i64 with Mut, Alloc {
    match current {
        None => 0
        Some(n) => 1
    }
}

fn main() -> i32 with IO, Mut, Panic, Div, Alloc {
    let node = Node { v: 42 }
    let items = Some(Box::new(node))
    if takes_one(items) == 1 && takes_two(items, None) == 1 {
        print("option box across call: PASS\n")
    }
    return 0
}
